+# NIST–Commission interview agreement and briefing record
+
+November 9, 2026 · STL01–STL03 / B188 / H005. A five-page Commission archive scan preserves the **October 28, 2003** three-party agreement on selected FDNY and NYPD interviews and a separate NYC–Commission side letter. It gives a dated source for the terms governing those interviews. A matched briefing deck and NIST's final report give separately scoped planning figures and accounts of what was collected. [Archive and source locators](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## What the signed pages establish
+
+| Instrument and locator | Scope stated on the page |
+| --- | --- |
+| Three-party agreement, item 135 physical p. 3, §§1–2 | Sets a maximum of **100 FDNY** and **25 NYPD** employees for the covered interviews. NIST conducts the interview and the Commission can ask follow-up questions. |
+| Same instrument, physical pp. 4–5, §§8–9 | Conditions taping and transcription of covered interviews on interviewee consent. Provides conditions for disclosing identifying information and says the two bodies will oppose requests for interview tapes, notes and other records. Additions or modifications require a signed writing. P. 5 shows three signature blocks with handwritten signatures. |
+| NYC–Commission side letter, physical pp. 1–2, §§1–5 | Preserves the Commission's ability to seek additional interviews and subpoena individuals. It records separate consent and disclosure terms for interviews under the letter, says the City has no duty under §1 to grant an additional-interview request, and states that its terms supersede conflicting terms in the main agreement. P. 2 shows the October 28 acceptance date and two signed blocks. |
+
+The scan preserves signed instruments with separate coverage and fax markings. The underlying dispatch, original execution files, changes and interview-by-interview implementation remain open. The agreements concern interview evidence. The earlier concern about who approved steel shipment, handling and disposition still requires the preservation and transfer records. [September 2003 steel preservation briefing](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/f105c8705611b17955e551039bc8f6b217c9b307/investigation/broader-evidence/steel-commission-preservation-meeting-2026-11-09/README.md)
+
+## Recorded, transcribed and authorized
+
+- The agreement conditions taping and transcription on consent. The final emergency response report says **most interviews were digitally recorded**, handwritten notes were taken throughout, and **only the Port Authority interviews used consent forms**. It separately says all NIST first-person interviews in this analysis were transcribed and describes **116 first-person interview documents**, **46** radio/telephone transcriptions and **650** other documents in a total of **812**. Preserve the condition, the form statement and the reported processing separately. The records available here leave the consent method and the source of each transcript open. An interview roster with non-identifying consent, audio and transcript fields could test this. [NCSTAR 1-8, printed pp. 12 and 39](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/NCSTAR/ncstar1-8v1.pdf)
+- The emergency response report says the Port Authority agreed to **12** personnel interviews, and its conducted-count table lists **15** Port Authority interviews. The towers final report describes agreement to interview **15**. Keep all three statements with their record roles. The original Port Authority authorization, any amendments and the interview accounting would test their relationship. The conducted counts **68 FDNY + 25 NYPD + 15 Port Authority + 8 other = 116**. The first two sum to **93**, beside the agreement's ceiling of **125** for covered NYC personnel. These are reported counts and authorization limits. An interview-level inventory would be needed to identify coverage of specific witness topics. [NCSTAR 1-8, §§2.2.3 and 5.1](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/NCSTAR/ncstar1-8v1.pdf) · [NCSTAR 1, §7.2.1, printed p. 163](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/NCSTAR/ncstar1.pdf)
+
